\chapter{The Foveated Streaming Backend}\label{ch:foveated}

On visionOS 26.4 and later, Foveated Streaming is a system framework: there is no package to add,
only [[import FoveatedStreaming]], an entitlement, and a server that speaks Apple's session
protocol. On iOS, Apple's StreamingSession package offers the same types without the eye tracking.
This chapter adapts the session, presents the connection flow, and then the build configuration.

\section{Endpoints}

A foveated session connects to an \emph{endpoint}. [[FoveatedEndpointKind]] lists the three kinds
the form offers:
\begin{description}[style=nextline]
\item[System discovery] The system browses the local network for servers advertising
  [[_apple-foveated-streaming._tcp]], lets the user pick one, and pairs with it by scanning a QR
  code the server displays; the code carries a client token and the server's certificate
  fingerprint.
\item[Local address] A server at a known IP address and port; the session-management protocol
  listens on port 55000 by default.
\item[Remote server] A cloud deployment reached by name through a signaling service.
\end{description}
The enumeration and the default port are compiled in every build because the form's stored
settings use them; everything that touches the framework is inside [[FOVEATED_STREAMING]].

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/Streaming/FoveatedSession.swift>>=
<<MIT permission notice>>
// Copyright (c) 2026 Pablo Figueroa and Claude.

import Foundation
import Network
#if FOVEATED_STREAMING
#if os(visionOS)
import FoveatedStreaming
#else
import StreamingSession
#endif
#endif

/// How the foveated session finds its server.
enum FoveatedEndpointKind: String, CaseIterable {
    /// The system browses for `_apple-foveated-streaming._tcp` servers and pairs with a QR code.
    case discover = "Discover & pair (QR code)"
    /// A server on the local network at a known address.
    case local = "Manual IP address"
    /// A cloud server reached through a signaling service.
    case remote = "Remote server"
}

extension FoveatedEndpointKind {
    /// The default port of the foveated streaming session-management protocol.
    static let defaultPort = 55000
}

@
[[endpoint(hostAddress:port:serverName:)]] turns the form's fields into the framework's
[[Endpoint]], validating the address (IPv4 or IPv6), the port and the server name. Errors carry a
message the form can show.

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/Streaming/FoveatedSession.swift>>=
#if FOVEATED_STREAMING
struct FoveatedEndpointError: LocalizedError {
    let errorDescription: String?
}

extension FoveatedEndpointKind {

    func endpoint(hostAddress: String, port: Int, serverName: String) throws -> FoveatedStreamingSession.Endpoint {
        switch self {
        case .discover:
            return .systemDiscovered
        case .local:
            let host = hostAddress.trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespacesAndNewlines)
            let ipAddress: any IPAddress
            if let ipv4 = IPv4Address(host) {
                ipAddress = ipv4
            } else if let ipv6 = IPv6Address(host) {
                ipAddress = ipv6
            } else {
                throw FoveatedEndpointError(errorDescription: "\"\(host)\" is not a valid IP address")
            }
            guard (1...Int(UInt16.max)).contains(port), let nwPort = NWEndpoint.Port(rawValue: UInt16(port)) else {
                throw FoveatedEndpointError(errorDescription: "\(port) is not a valid port")
            }
            return .local(ipAddress: ipAddress, port: nwPort)
        case .remote:
            let name = serverName.trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespacesAndNewlines)
            guard !name.isEmpty else {
                throw FoveatedEndpointError(errorDescription: "Enter a server name")
            }
            return .remote(serverName: name, signalingHeaders: [:])
        }
    }
}

@
\section{The session}

Unlike CloudXRKit's session, [[FoveatedStreamingSession]] conforms to [[ViewerSession]] directly:
it is already main-actor isolated and already has [[disconnect()]], [[pause()]] and [[resume()]]
with the protocol's signatures, so the extension only adds the status mapping, the description and
the channels. A disconnection the application asked for is not an error;
[[endpointInitiatedDisconnect]], [[unauthorized]] and any other reason are.

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/Streaming/FoveatedSession.swift>>=
extension FoveatedStreamingSession: ViewerSession {
    var viewerStatus: ViewerStatus {
        switch status {
        case .initialized:
            .initialized
        case .connecting:
            .connecting
        case .connected:
            .connected
        case .pausing:
            .pausing
        case .paused:
            .paused
        case .resuming:
            .resuming
        case .disconnecting:
            .disconnecting
        case .disconnected(let reason):
            .disconnected(error: reason == .appInitiatedDisconnect ? nil : reason.localizedDescription)
        @unknown default:
            .initialized
        }
    }

@
The framework returns the channel identifiers as a set on visionOS and as an array on
iOS; sorting the mapped identifiers by label gives the picker a stable order on both.

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/Streaming/FoveatedSession.swift>>=
    var statusDescription: String {
        status.description
    }

    var channelIDs: [ViewerChannelID] {
        availableMessageChannels
            .map { ViewerChannelID(label: "Channel \($0.label)", key: $0) }
            .sorted { $0.label < $1.label }
    }

    func channel(for id: ViewerChannelID) -> (any ViewerChannel)? {
        guard let channelID = id.key.base as? MessageChannel.ID else {
            return nil
        }
        return messageChannel(for: channelID)
    }
}

@
The text form of a channel
identifier, [[stringRepresentation]], appeared in visionOS 27. The deployment target is visionOS
26.4, the first release with Foveated Streaming, so on older systems the label falls back to the
identifier's description. The iOS package has the property without an availability limit.

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/Streaming/FoveatedSession.swift>>=
private extension FoveatedStreamingSession.MessageChannel.ID {
    var label: String {
#if os(visionOS)
        // visionOS 26 has no public text form of a channel ID.
        if #available(visionOS 27.0, *) {
            return stringRepresentation
        }
        return String(describing: self)
#else
        return stringRepresentation
#endif
    }
}

@
The framework's channel
already has the stream and a throwing send; the extension adds the description.

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/Streaming/FoveatedSession.swift>>=
extension FoveatedStreamingSession.MessageChannel: ViewerChannel {
    var statusDescription: String {
        switch channelStatus {
        case .ready:
            "ready"
        case .closed:
            "closed"
        @unknown default:
            "unknown"
        }
    }

    func send(_ data: Data) throws {
        try sendMessage(data)
    }
}
#endif
